        <div id="total-vs-per-port" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Total Wattage vs Per-Port Wattage: The Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Big Number Misleads</h3>

          <p>
            Every multi charging station is sold on a single big number, and it is usually the wrong number to shop on. A charger labeled 100W might deliver 100W to one laptop on its own, but the moment you plug in a phone it may drop that laptop to 65W, and if you fill every port it may hand out as little as 15W to each. The total tells you the size of the power budget. The per-port rating tells you how fast any single device can charge. You need both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With Your Hungriest Device</h3>

          <p>
            Start with your hungriest device. A 13-inch laptop typically wants 30W to 65W, a 14-inch around 65W to 100W, and a 16-inch performance laptop can use 140W through USB-C PD 3.1. A current phone charges at 20W to 45W depending on the brand, a tablet at 20W to 45W, and earbuds, watches and mice need 5W to 10W. If the station cannot deliver your laptop's wattage through at least one port, the laptop will charge slowly or, under load, barely hold its level.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Up What Charges at Once</h3>

          <p>
            Then add up what charges at the same time. Two laptops at 65W, two phones at 25W and a tablet at 20W adds up to 200W. A 100W station in that situation will not fail, but it will ration power, and every device charges slower. That is fine overnight, when nobody cares whether a phone takes one hour or three. It is not fine at a desk where the laptop is in use and draining as fast as it charges.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
