        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best charging station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most desks, the Anker Prime Charger 200W is the best charging station. It has four USB-C and two USB-A ports, 200W of total output and up to 100W from any USB-C port, so two laptops and two phones charge at full speed. Add a 3-in-1 Qi2 stand for the nightstand if you use an iPhone and Apple Watch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a multi charging station and a power strip?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A multi charging station focuses on USB ports, usually four to six, with enough wattage to fast-charge laptops and phones. A power strip provides AC outlets for plugs. Charging power strips such as the Anker 7-in-1 combine both, but their USB budget is smaller, so they suit nightstands and single-laptop desks better than busy shared desks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 3-in-1 charging station worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3-in-1 charging station is worth it if you own an iPhone, an Apple Watch and AirPods and want them charged on a nightstand every morning without cables. Buy a Qi2-certified model with Apple Watch fast charging. It is not worth it for Android phones with non-Apple watches, where compatibility is patchy and a simple cable is faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts should a charging station have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the devices that charge at the same time and add 20 percent. A phone, watch and earbuds need about 40W. One laptop plus accessories needs about 100W. Two laptops and two phones need about 200W. A 16-inch performance laptop needs a 140W USB-C port, which only a few stations, like the Anker Prime 8-in-1, provide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap multi-device charging stations safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are and many are not. No-name family docks often carry only CE or FCC marks, overstate wattage and skip proper protection circuits. Look for a UL, ETL or CSA listing, buy from the brand or an authorized seller, and avoid prices far below everyone else's. A charging station runs overnight with batteries attached, so certification matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a charging station work during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it has its own battery. A wall-powered charging station stops working when the grid goes down. A portable charging station like the Anker SOLIX C300 DC, with 288Wh of LiFePO4 storage, keeps phones and laptops charging for days, and it can be recharged from a car socket or a folding solar panel during a long outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do GaN chargers damage phone batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. GaN only changes how the charger converts power, not how much the phone accepts. The phone controls its own charging speed through USB-C Power Delivery, so a 100W port will not force more into a phone than it requests. GaN chargers run cooler than old silicon bricks, which, if anything, is slightly better for battery health.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping on total wattage alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 100W label usually means 100W from one port, not 100W to each. Plug in a laptop and a phone and the laptop may drop to 65W. Check the per-port figure for your biggest device, then check that the total covers everything you charge at the same time, with roughly 20 percent headroom left over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a no-name family dock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The family dock category is crowded with unbranded boxes promising eight ports and huge wattage. Many lack UL, ETL or CSA listings and deliver far less than claimed. A dock runs overnight with several batteries attached, so buy from a reputable brand even if it means fewer slots for the money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeding a 3-in-1 stand from an old adapter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Qi2 stand needs a capable USB-C power adapter, often 30W to 45W. Run it from an old 5W or 12W cube and every device charges at a crawl, and some stands will not charge the phone at all. Check what comes in the box and buy the right adapter if the stand does not include one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging heaters into a charging strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A charging power strip is still a power strip. Space heaters, kettles, hair dryers and microwaves draw far more current than these strips are built for, and they belong in a wall outlet. Keep charging strips for lamps, monitors, routers and chargers, and never daisy-chain one strip from another to gain outlets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a wall station to help in a blackout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every desktop charger, 3-in-1 stand and family dock stops working the instant the grid goes down. If outages are a real concern where you live, keep a charged power bank or a portable charging station with its own battery, and top it up every two to three months so it is full when needed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

